[232.556223, "o", "     1\t## Investigation Brief 02 — Status Precedence\r\n     2\t\r\n     3\tAuditor directive (mandatory for extract):\r\n     4\t\r\n     5\tInterview notes with the compliance desk confirmed status fights between mail,\r\n     6\tamendments, and corrections. Ledger rows provide the base status (lowercase on\r\n     7\toutput). Status precedence from lowest to highest is: pending, approved,\r\n     8\trejected, reversed. Email excerpts may change status only when the From line\r\n     9\tcontains `compliance@` anywhere in the address; ignore all other senders.\r\n    10\t\r\n    11\t## Investigation Brief 02 — Status Precedence — field notes\r\n    12\t\r\n    13\tRisk assessment WP-2138 ties capital project capitalization to control deficiencies around mail ingestion. Teams must not treat decoy ledger headings or appendix commentary as authoritative transaction sources.\r\n    14\t\r\n    15\tWorking paper WP-3210 documents a three-way match failure tied to capital project capitalization. The team escalated when repeated extractor runs produc"]
[232.556676, "o", "ed different status columns for the same transaction id.\r\n    16\t\r\n    17\tDraft guidance in this paragraph is non-binding: some early tooling compared compliance mail against post-correction effective dates when flagging holds. The mid-year amendment later in this archive supersedes that draft practice.\r\n    18\t\r\n    19\tWorking paper WP-6073 documents a three-way match failure tied to capital project capitalization. The team escalated when repeated extractor runs produced different status columns for the same transaction id.\r\n    20\t\r\n    21\tLead reviewer J. Huang noted that capital project capitalization created reconciliation noise in fixed assets during July review. Staff initially blamed tooling, but timeline reconstruction showed manual overrides arriving after batch lock.\r\n    22\t\r\n    23\tThe committee packet references capital project capitalization as a contributing factor to late compliance holds. Investigators cross-checked mail excerpts against amendment minutes before accepting any owner change.\r\n"]
[232.556917, "o", "    24\t\r\n    25\tLead reviewer L. Bergstrom noted that capital project capitalization created reconciliation noise in fixed assets during July review. Staff initially blamed tooling, but timeline reconstruction showed manual overrides arriving after batch lock.\r\n    26\t\r\n    27\tFollow-up #238 confirmed that fixed assets had been using an informal spreadsheet for capital project capitalization. The formal extractor must instead derive rows only from the canonical ledger and structured sections at file end.\r\n    28\t\r\n    29\tWorking paper WP-3893 documents a three-way match failure tied to capital project capitalization. The team escalated when repeated extractor runs produced different status columns for the same transaction id.\r\n    30\t\r\n    31\tIn July review, external advisors reviewed capital project capitalization and asked for a machine-readable reconciliation trace. The archive preserves narrative context so auditors can explain why a given transaction received compliance_hold.\r\n    32\t\r\n    33\tMemo WP-288"]
